<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aapravasi Ghat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aapravasi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a> is the former immigration depot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, where indentured labourers arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were received from 1849 until the early 20th century. Nearly half a million workers passed through Mauritius under the indenture system after the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, shaping its population. The surviving buildings became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aapravasi-ghat.jpg" alt="Aapravasi Ghat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aapravasi_Ghat_Museum,_Mauritius_(41).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Immigration Depot was built on the east side of the sheltered bay of Trou Fanfaron in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>. The name Aapravasi Ghat, in use since 1987, is a direct Hindi translation of Immigration Depot: aapravasi means immigrant, and ghat can mean a landing place or interface between land and water. An older name was the Coolie Ghat. Land reclamation has since moved the site inland, and the Caudan Waterfront lies beyond it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in 1721, landing at Trou Fanfaron, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations worked by slaves had developed by the mid-18th century. Mauritius passed to British control in 1810. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in European colonies in 1834, the British government sought labourers from other parts of the world, and the indenture scheme, known as the Great Experiment, brought workers under contract in return for passage, basic accommodation and a small wage. Mauritius was its focus, as its plantation economy was still expanding.</p><p>From 1834 to 1849 no fixed depot existed. In 1849 a building from the French period was chosen as the core of a permanent depot, which was enlarged until 1857 and could hold up to 1,000 labourers at once. Migrants stayed in the immigration shed for up to three days before distribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estates or onward transport to other colonies. A railway of 1865 cut the depot in two, and a stone-arched gateway was built the same year. Beet sugar competition and a malaria epidemic in the 1860s reduced immigration, which ceased in 1923. An estimated 450,000 indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had passed through the depot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only partial remains of three stone buildings from the 1860s survive: the entrance gateway and a hospital block, remnants of the immigration sheds and traces of the service quarters. The 14 stone steps from the wharf are intact. After 1923 the buildings were put to other uses, and a bus station and motorway in the 1970s led to demolitions. The remains were protected as a national monument in 1987, and the Aapravasi Ghat Trust Fund was established in 2001 to manage the site. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2006.</p><p>Most of the workers came from what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. Indians made up less than four percent of the island's population in 1835 and more than 66 percent by 1860; about 68 percent of Mauritians have Indian ancestry. A national holiday on 2 November commemorates the arrival of the indentured labourers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aapravasi Ghat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After slavery ended in 1835, nearly half a million indentured labourers arrived from India through the immigration depot now called <b>Aapravasi Ghat</b>, so that Hindus and Muslims of Indian origin became the majority while Creoles and French-speaking whites kept much economic power.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a></li></ul></section>
